Objective: {{task}}

Step 3: settle the field set. Write the complete field list you would defend as final, each with its type and its godoc comment, and report it. Report every field the struct has, not only the ones you just added.

Choose `revise` if you want to look at the result and judge it again. Choose `confirmed` only when the set is final.

Objective: {{task}}

Step 6: implement exactly one body, following the plan you wrote for it. One body per step: leave the others panicking. Report which function you implemented and where the implementation departed from its plan. Do not paste the code into the payload.

Choose `next_body` while functions remain unimplemented, `implemented` when none do, and `another_struct` only if implementing revealed that a second type is genuinely required.

Objective: {{task}}

Step 7: pin the behaviour down. Write the tests for what you just built, in the test file beside the code. Test what a reader would doubt, not what a signature already promises: the boundaries, the error paths, and anything concurrent. Every test runs without sleeping.

Report one entry per test that now exists, each with what it establishes. Do not paste test code into the payload. The gate runs the tests, so you cannot leave this step until they pass.

Objective: {{task}}

Step 8: the deterministic gate. Read the finished code and its tests once more. Answer `clean` only if the code is finished, every exported identifier carries a godoc comment starting with its name, the package documentation describes what the package actually is, and nothing introduces package-level mutable state.

This step collects no payload. If something is wrong, send the walk back to the step that can fix it: `repair_body` for a wrong body, `more_tests` for a behaviour nothing pins down yet. Escape only when the defect is structural and neither step can reach it.
